 \begin{abstract}
-A motion repertoire is useful for traversal only when the robot can execute a
-suitable alternative and select it from information available before commitment.
-We develop Motion2Scene, an execution-aware curriculum for humanoid overhead
-traversal with a frozen low-level tracker. Executed body envelopes construct
-solution-preserving geometric proposals; complete physical schedule branches
-supply passage-first, measured-time teaching targets. A 114-dimensional causal
-floor/ceiling-history policy can wait at three legal phases before one adaptation
-and recovery. Historical replay and information-consistent teaching are evaluated
-as separate development extensions. In 42 forced-schedule simulation episodes on six
-development contexts, seven qualified schedules provide a passing alternative
-for every context; the strongest constant passes five. A prior-derived option
-and an authored sustained option solve complementary constraints. A 36-episode
-policy comparison on the same six development contexts shows that adding the
-complementary teaching data raises learned passage from five to six contexts,
-matching a script retuned on the same data. Both updated policies take 0.50 s
-longer on each of two previously solved passages. These results establish a
-constructive option-and-data extension with a measured efficiency tradeoff;
-independent curriculum benefit and held-out improvement remain open.
+Motion-conditioned scene construction can concentrate physical labeling on
+encounters that require adaptation without teaching a policy to select between
+adaptations. We examine this distinction for humanoid overhead traversal using
+a frozen tracker, seven qualified schedules, and a causal three-phase selector.
+In a completed five-arm development comparison, executed-contrast construction
+and feasibility-screened uniform each pass five of six contexts at each of
+three acquisition seeds. Thirteen of fifteen learned policies choose a constant
+schedule across these contexts. Historical replay changes the fitted parameters
+but not the executed decisions. A retrospective screen audit recovers two of
+seven measured response-complementarity positives; its two selected positives
+do not establish population precision. These comparisons are consistent with
+positive-support filtering explaining part of the construction advantage over
+unscreened placement, but do not isolate a sole causal mechanism. A separately
+registered support-broadening pilot has completed acquisition; its validation
+panel remains incomplete after an interrupted execution. We report that status
+without drawing a pilot utility conclusion. The evidence motivates separating
+proposal geometry, physical labels, and downstream decision utility, while
+establishing neither population equivalence nor held-out or hardware benefit.
 \end{abstract}
 
 \section{Introduction}
-A humanoid may track diverse motions yet fail to use them in a constrained room.
-An overhead passage requires a whole-body transition early enough to clear its
-front edge, a sufficiently low executed shape throughout its length, and a legal
-return to locomotion. A reference pose fitting beneath the obstacle establishes
-none of these conditions. A training scene also provides little decision value
-when its relevant sensor evidence arrives after the useful transition.
-
-The teaching problem is to expose decisions that matter. An obstacle cleared by
-every motion may teach timing preferences but not the need to change body shape;
-an obstacle defeating every motion cannot be solved by improving selection alone.
-Our approach constructs constraints from the swept body geometry of executed
-alternatives. A useful contrast leaves one schedule geometrically clear while
-intersecting another, concentrating physical labeling on candidate decisions
-where the available responses differ.
-
-We retain the frozen SONIC tracker~\cite{sonic} and expose qualified
-entry--reference--return schedules built from generated and authored motions.
-The selector learns when to commit from egocentric floor and ceiling history.
-Crucially, WAIT retains later choices: its target must evaluate a complete
-continuation, and that continuation must be selectable from the information
-available at the later decision. Figure~\ref{fig:complementary_execution} shows
-why selecting between alternatives matters: the prior-derived and sustained
-responses solve different constraints.
-
-Motion-conditioned scene construction has precedent in Learning from
-Hallucination (LfH), Learning from Learned Hallucination (LfLH), and
-LfH-CP~\cite{lfh,lflh,lfhcp}; PAIRED and ACCEL guide environment design through
-performance gaps~\cite{paired,accel}, while Prioritized Level Replay selects
-previous environments according to estimated learning potential~\cite{plr}.
-Motion2Scene links construction and learning through measured humanoid
-execution. Executed contrasts propose scenes, complete physical continuations
-teach commitment, and pre-update student outcomes provide a separate replay
-signal. The geometric candidate queues remain fixed throughout acquisition.
-
-Our technical contributions are execution-conditioned contrast construction and
-complete-continuation supervision for multi-phase perceptive commitment. We also
-formulate an observation-consistent teacher on the finite decision tree and
-compare replay signals within a common learner. Experiments distinguish the
-effects of extending physical capability, acquiring teaching encounters and
-reweighting those encounters. The completed development results establish
-complementary responses and a closed-loop teaching-data benefit with a time
-regression. The broader acquisition study tests whether construction and
-supervision produce a repeatable downstream advantage.
