-\subsection{Qualified executions construct candidate scenes}
-Qualification verifies each complete schedule from the relevant neutral approach,
-including entry, maintained adaptation and return. Its measured native body poses
-transform conservative outer primitives and inner primitive witnesses. We
-construct scenes using these executed envelopes, not reference heights. For a
-positive schedule $s^+$ and a negative alternative $s^-$, the analytic screen is
-\begin{equation}
- \min_{\delta\in\U}c^{\mathrm{outer}}_{s^+}(q\oplus\delta)\ge m,
- \qquad c^{\mathrm{inner}}_{s^-}(q)\le -m.
- \label{eq:screen}
-\end{equation}
-The six-second development screen uses $m=0.01$ m and 81 combinations of world
-$x/y$ offsets $\{-0.02,0,0.02\}$ m, underside offsets $\{-0.005,0,0.005\}$ m,
-and yaw offsets $\{-0.02,0,0.02\}$ rad. Positive clearance is required across
-this finite set; negative interference is required only at nominal placement.
-These are sampled geometric proposal conditions, not physical robustness
-certificates. Obstacles can change the subsequently executed trajectory.
-
-The primary candidate pool is drawn before acquisition outcomes. Target-only
-assigns its positive schedule by a frozen random draw before geometric queries.
-Uniform uses the first robust positive in the original draw order, while analytic
-contrast searches distinct positive/negative pairs and maximizes minimum slack.
-These queues differ in geometric eligibility; target-only does not use a negative
-outcome. Common-support quotas select four distinct
-available scene strata per corpus in frozen queue order; no physical failure
-causes replacement or geometry re-selection. The analytic and observation
-curriculum arms use exactly the same candidate IDs, order and teacher schedules,
-so that their comparison isolates replay under the common acquisition design.
-
-The reference-envelope comparator applies native URDF forward kinematics to
-the commanded full schedules, including entry and return, and uses identical
-collision shapes, candidate scenes, margins and placement offsets. We first
-check this kinematic map against native body poses using recorded root and joint
-states. The comparison then changes only commanded versus executed body poses;
-both constructors receive the same complete physical labeling and downstream
-learner.
-
-\subsection{Causal observation and complete-continuation teaching}
-The student receives 114 named values: 100 sensor/history, phase and robot-state
-features, seven preaction active-schedule indicators and seven legality entries.
-Sixty-five ideal mounted PhysX rays and normals accumulate for at most two
-seconds or 101 frames in an egocentric floor/ceiling representation with separate
-observed/unknown masks. Scene parameters, object identities, physical labels and
-postdecision actions are excluded. This is an ideal simulated range interface,
-not a noisy depth-camera result or dense vertical-free-space guarantee.
-
-Each scene is executed under all seven forced schedules with a matched physics
-seed. Before a teacher branch can label a decision, its recorded state, prior
-actions, reference tokens, causal sensor history, feature vector and legality
-must match the forced-neutral branch's preaction prefix exactly. This supplies
-bootstrap targets even without an actual student episode. Matching an actual
-student's visited neutral prefix is an additional requirement for gap-based
-replay below. For action $a$ at phase $k$, let $\mathcal S_k(a)$ be all registered
-complete schedules sharing
-that immediate decision. A current commitment has its corresponding schedule;
-WAIT includes neutral continuation and every legal later-entry schedule. A
-complete WAIT target therefore cannot be replaced by the forced-walking outcome.
-
-Every expected continuation must have an admitted outcome before its immediate
-action table is complete. From a complete table with a successful continuation,
-\begin{equation}
- s_k^\star=\arg\min_{s\in\bigcup_a\mathcal S_k(a),\ \tau_s\text{ passes}}T_s.
-\end{equation}
-The immediate teacher action is the first action of $s_k^\star$. An action's value
-uses its best passing complete continuation, or its measured failure if none
-passes. Missing branches and unavailable later phases are not failures. Tables
-without a successful continuation provide no fitting target. Ordinarily, fitting
-also requires consequential passage or cost differences. A narrow measured-tie
-initialization rule below handles an otherwise unsupported phase; all physical
-recordings and costs remain in the data.
-
-The common student fits a separate linear ridge head at each legal phase to
-negative measured regret. For a complete decision table, failure has regret one;
-a passing action has
-\begin{equation}
- R_k(a)=\frac{T_k(a)-T_k^\star}
- {\max_{b\in\A_k,\ b\ \mathrm{passes}}T_k(b)}.
-\end{equation}
-Per-phase input normalization uses a standard-deviation floor of 0.05; intercepts
-are unpenalized. Regression weights are normalized per legal action. Runtime
-scores are negative predicted regrets: masked score argmax selects the legal
-action with minimum predicted regret. These
-unconstrained estimates are not success probabilities, and regression does not
-preserve the teacher's lexicographic success preference as a guarantee. The
-common penalty $\lambda=10$ was selected on five development layouts from a
-registered finite sweep, before primary acquisition.
-
-At bootstrap, a phase with only complete, equally successful and equal-time
-alternatives receives zero regret targets and deterministic tie breaking. Once
-consequential examples arrive, they determine that phase's fit. The exact rule
-and its development history appear in Appendix~\ref{app:initialization}.
-
-\subsection{Observation-consistent continuation supervision}
-Scene-wise schedule search can overvalue WAIT when later successful actions
-depend on information unavailable to the student. We implement a finite
-information-group teacher to test and address this failure. Let $I_k$ contain
-encounters with exactly equal available 114-dimensional student vectors at phase
-$k$. For this dynamic program, successive partitions must refine: later groups
-must retain earlier distinctions. Inputs that violate this condition require
-an explicit memory representation or a different policy solver.
-
-Starting at the last phase, retain every legal commitment that passes in all
-members of $I_k$. Retain WAIT only when every successor group admits a common
-passing continuation. In notation,
-\begin{equation}
- \A_k^+(I_k)=\{a\in\A_k^{\mathrm{commit}}:\ Y_i(a)=1\ \forall i\in I_k\}
- \ \cup\ \{\mathrm{WAIT}:\A_{k+1}^+(J)\ne\varnothing\ \forall J\subseteq I_k\},
-\end{equation}
-where $J$ ranges over successor information groups; at the last phase WAIT
-means the complete neutral schedule. $Y_i(a)$ is the measured full-schedule
-outcome. Incomplete continuation tables withhold supervision. Among passing
-policies, choose the one minimizing mean measured passage time within the group;
-retain all passing actions and all exact time minimizers as set-valued targets.
-This enforces equal actions for equivalent information while allowing later
-observations to distinguish tasks.
-
-When no common policy passes the entire group, the teacher maximizes attainable
-passage count $N_a$ before minimizing the successful-time sum $C_a$. A commitment
-uses its measured schedule; WAIT sums the optimal successor-group values.
-Backward induction retains all equal optima under uniform empirical encounter
-mass. Let $N^*=\max_a N_a$ and let $C_*,C^*$ be the minimum and maximum costs
-among actions attaining $N^*$. For $N^*>0$, shared regret targets are
-\begin{equation}
- R_I(a)=\begin{cases}
- (N^*-N_a)/N^*,&N_a<N^*,\\
- (C_a-C_*)/(N^*C^*),&N_a=N^*.
- \end{cases}
-\end{equation}
-Positive passage times make every equal-count time regret smaller than losing
-one passage. Singleton groups recover the original regret exactly. These
-explicit targets train the same ridge equations; unknown or all-failing groups
-provide no passing supervision. The teacher is a development extension;
-the ongoing acquisition comparison retains its original scene-wise teacher.
-
-\subsection{Historical physical gaps prioritize replay}
-At acquisition round $r$, the student model $M_{r-1}$ is committed with exactly
-bootstrap and earlier-round teacher data before the current student's scene is
-executed. All seven current teacher branches follow that actual student episode.
-This order prevents current-scene labels from entering the model used to measure
-its gap. Every arm uses the same student-before-teachers encounter structure
-and branch budget.
-
-For a matched neutral phase with a complete feasible teacher, compare the actual
-full student outcome with the teacher's best continuation:
-\begin{equation}
- g_{r,k}=\begin{cases}
- 1,&\text{verified student failure},\\
- \max\!\left(0,\dfrac{T_{M_{r-1}}-T_k^\star}
- {\max(T_{M_{r-1}},T_k^\star)}\right),&\text{student passes}.
- \end{cases}
-\end{equation}
-Unverified, unmatched or unavailable outcomes receive no gap priority. Recorded
-ray endpoints are associated with beam geometry only for training-time cue scoring.
-The primary rule requires each enabled beam's surface cue to have been delivered
-by the current decision's physical timestamp. It does not require an underside
-return and does not prove that two tasks are distinguishable. A future-entry cue
-is separately recorded and is never credited as current information. Safe WAIT
-without a current cue remains eligible for ordinary teacher training, but receives
-no regret priority under this rule.
-
-Replay mixes 0.2 uniform, 0.2 declared option/length/phase/entry/return coverage,
-and 0.6 verified-gap mass. We normalize encounter mass before distributing it
-across supervised phases, preventing extra WAIT opportunities from tripling an
-encounter's weight. An encounter's gap is the maximum of its eligible phase gaps.
-When all gaps are zero or unavailable, only the 0.6 gap component falls back to
-uniform: the resulting mixture is \emph{0.8 uniform plus 0.2 coverage}. Missing
-supervision gets zero fitting weight, not a fabricated label.
-
-Coverage uses a joint key consisting of the best continuation's reference,
-ordered enabled-beam length bins, current phase, entry tick and return tick.
-Length edges are $\{0.25,0.5,1,2\}$ m; an exact edge belongs to the upper bin.
-Let $n_b$ count encounters containing key $b$, counted once per encounter, and
-$v_{ik}=1/n_{b(i,k)}$ for its supervised phases $K_i$. Define
-\begin{equation}
- u_i=\frac{1}{|K_i|}\sum_{k\in K_i}v_{ik},\qquad
- p^{\mathrm{cov}}_{ik}=\frac{u_i}{\sum_j u_j}
-                      \frac{v_{ik}}{\sum_{\ell\in K_i}v_{i\ell}}.
-\end{equation}
-The dimensions form one joint bin rather than separately averaged marginal
-distributions. The coverage term contributes $0.2p^{\mathrm{cov}}_{ik}$.
-
-A historical gap retains its generating model and round age. Our development
-controls compare uniform mass, coverage alone, their 0.8/0.2 mixture, and refreshed
-supervised-error priority. The latter replaces gap priority by the mean squared
-residual between predicted and measured regret across legal actions, recomputed
-after each fit. It uses the same encounter/phase normalization and fallback as
-historical replay. This is a cheap fitting signal; physical gaps continue to
-require actual executions of their generating models.
-
-\section{Experiments}
-All completed experiments below use development data: six fixed traversal
-contexts, independently acquired teaching encounters, and separate motion-option
-and perception studies. The six fixed contexts test empty, short, sustained,
-early-commitment, two-beam and complementary passages. Physical schedule tables,
-new closed-loop policy executions and recorded-input readouts are reported
-separately. The common acquisition learner is the phase-specific ridge model
-with penalty 10; the tracker and seven-schedule bank remain fixed. Larger
-acquisition checkpoints and reserved evaluation are described at the end of
-this section. Appendix~\ref{app:data} documents the reusable recordings.
-
-\subsection{Does execution change the constructed teaching constraints?}
-We extend the shared geometric pool to 3,840 candidates: 256 draws per stratum,
-five strata and three seeds. Native forward kinematics reproduces recorded body
-positions within 2 micrometres, supporting a controlled reference-versus-executed
-envelope comparison. Table~\ref{tab:construction_geometry} shows that only
-169 of 645 reference-selected positive/negative pairs retain the same geometric
-contrast under measured execution. The chosen positive retains robust clearance
-in 182 cases; the chosen negative retains nominal intersection in 624. Thus the
-main discrepancy is whether the proposed passing response retains its clearance.
-Nevertheless, 207 reference-selected scenes have some executed contrast, possibly
-using a different pair. Complete physical teacher branches remain necessary to
-measure useful-task yield and downstream passage.
-
