-\subsection{Can the learner retain capability without the cost regression?}
-\paragraph{Information-consistent continuation test.}
-We first isolate a limitation that no learner can overcome without timely
-information. Mask all 28 corridor-summary features before a declared reveal
-decision, retaining recorded proprioception and legal actions. The remaining
-features are exactly equal across the six contexts at all three phases.
-With scene information available from 0.30 s or 1.00 s, an exact finite
-observation-group policy can pass all six contexts. Revealing it only at 1.40 s,
-or never, reduces the optimum to five, although the scene-wise schedule bank
-still covers six. At a 1.00 s reveal, the shared initial action is WAIT. At a
-1.40 s reveal, the early context has lost its last prior commitment opportunity;
-no earlier common commitment also passes the complementary context. This
-recorded-table information ablation tests the teacher's shared-action constraint;
-it does not simulate sensor latency or a new closed-loop rollout.
-
-To separate the information value of WAIT from repertoire size, a matched
-control chooses once at 0.30 s among all seven complete schedules, including
-late-entry schedules. It executes neutral until the chosen entry. Within each
-initial information group, the control selects the schedule maximizing measured
-passage count, then minimizing summed successful passage time. Sequential
-selection can additionally condition later choices on newly delivered information.
-With corridor features revealed at 1.00 s, their finite passage limits are 5/6
-and 6/6, respectively. With information present initially both reach 6/6; with
-reveal at 1.40 s or never both reach 5/6. These exact recorded-table limits retain
-the same action repertoire and all six assigned contexts. They identify when
-later information can help; they do not measure a learned or newly executed
-policy, and nominal singleton groups yield no demonstrated WAIT advantage.
-
-Training the same ridge learner with group-consistent rather than scene-wise
-targets yields identical nominal coefficients. Both teachers produce 6/6
-passing branch proxies with information by 1.00 s and 5/6 with later or absent
-information. In the latter conditions, group-consistent targets select the
-earlier prior, reducing mean recorded time by 0.004 s over the same five successes,
-but not recovering the complementary passage. Thus this development comparison
-establishes trainable information-consistent targets, not a passage advantage.
-
-The alternative depth-two feasibility/time trees reduced fitted regret but
-passed only 4/6 actual development contexts, versus ridge's 6/6, and were
-0.12 s slower on their four mutually successful contexts. We therefore retain
-the common ridge learner. Appendix~\ref{app:learner_debug} records the fixed-data
-control, native failures and observed input sensitivity.
-
